\\sum.description:
Represents the sum over all possible basis states (eigenstates) of the operator 
\hat{A}
.

|n\\rangle.description:
The n-th orthonormal eigenstate (or eigenvector) of the operator 
\hat{A}
. It represents a specific quantum state of the system.

time-difference-45210654.interpretation:
The formula 
\Delta t = \frac{2L}{c} \frac{1}{1-v^2/c^2} - \frac{2L}{c} \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}}
 calculates the time difference 
\Delta t
 between two light paths in a moving frame. The first term, \(\frac{2L}{c} \frac{1}{1-v^2/c^2}\), represents the time taken for light to travel back and forth along one arm of an interferometer aligned with the direction of motion, given the length 
\(L
) of the arm and the speed of light 
\(c
). The second term, \(\frac{2L}{c} \frac{1}{\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}}\), represents the time taken for light to travel back and forth along the arm perpendicular to the direction of motion. These expressions are derived using the principles of special relativity, particularly the transformation of time and space coordinates. The factor \(\frac{1}{\sqrt{1-v^2/c^2}} = \gamma\) is the Lorentz factor, and \(\frac{1}{1-v^2/c^2}\) appears in the calculation for the arm parallel to the motion. The difference 
\(\Delta t\) is therefore the observable consequence of the constancy of the speed of light in all inertial frames, leading to time dilation and length contraction effects that influence the apparent travel time of light.
